// title: Secret endpoints: keeping a credential out of a subprocess
// desc: Point a child process at a loopback URL instead of the real API, so magus attaches the credential and the child never holds it. For your own code, magus\\secret.read is the ordinary choice.
// doc: concepts/secrets|Secrets
//
// There are two ways to give a build a credential, and you can stop at the first:
//
//   1. magus\secret.read - the value is a string in your magusfile, and magus knows
//      it IS a credential, so it is masked out of captured output, logs and the
//      cache. This is the ordinary choice for your own code.
//   2. magus\secret.endpoint - for a SUBPROCESS, which magus cannot redact. It
//      returns a loopback URL you point the child at instead of the real API.
//
// os\env is NOT on that list, and that is the one thing worth being firm about. With
// no provider selected, magus\secret.read("GITHUB_TOKEN") reads the same environment
// variable os\env would - the built-in environment provider IS a provider. Same
// variable, same plaintext, same effort. The difference is that magus then knows the
// value is a credential and can keep it out of what it writes down. Reading a
// credential with os\env costs you that for nothing in return. Keep os\env for
// things that are not credentials.
//
// Step 2 buys one thing: the child cannot leak a credential it never had. That matters
// when the child decides at runtime what to run. It buys nothing when you trust the
// consumer, which is why step 1 is not a lesser choice.
//
// What it does NOT buy: a child pointed at an endpoint can still SPEND the credential
// on any request the declaration permits. It stops exfiltration, not use.
//
// magus does NOT intercept traffic to do any of this: no generated certificate
// authority, nothing to install in a trust store, no decrypt-inspect-reencrypt. That
// is surveillance wearing security's clothes, and it fails its own threat model
// besides - see docs/concepts/secrets.md.
//
// Run `run agent-demo`.

import "magus";
import "os";
import "http";
import "proc";

// The shape, declared once - the same idiom as the registries step earlier in the
// tour. A grant says WHERE a credential lives and WHERE it may be sent, never what it
// is. Both halves are safe to commit.
//
// Declared HERE, the same way the registries step declares its Registry, rather than
// handed to you by magus. That is where the typing is real: Buzz checks the
// construction of an object it can see, so a misspelled field is a compile error.
// magus reads the four fields it needs off whatever you pass and validates them when
// the grant is registered.
object SecretGrant {
    ref: str = "",
    host: str = "",
    header: str = "",
    prefix: str = "",
}

final HTTPBIN = SecretGrant{
    ref    = "TOUR_API_TOKEN",
    host   = "httpbin.org",
    header = "Authorization",
    prefix = "Bearer ",
};

// The subprocess half. A child cannot be handed a Go HTTP client, so magus binds a
// loopback endpoint for the run and the child is pointed at it. There is no gateway to
// install and no service to start: the listener belongs to this run and closes when it
// ends.
//
// Note who decides which variable carries it. magus does NOT set one for you - you
// name it, because a tool with no base-URL knob should fail somewhere you can see it
// rather than silently talking to the real endpoint unauthenticated.
//
// The URL carries a random token, so another process on the machine cannot reach the
// port and spend your credential just by guessing a port number. TREAT THAT URL AS A
// CREDENTIAL: it is a bearer token in a query-shaped costume. Pass it through the
// environment, never in argv - a command line is world-readable on Linux
// (/proc/<pid>/cmdline), which is strictly worse than the environment you were trying
// to keep it out of.
//
// os\withEnv sets the variable for the callback and restores it after, so the value
// exists for exactly the span the child runs in. (ctx.withEnv is the sibling for a
// SPELL op, where you pass the returned context in: go["go-test"](ctx.withEnv({...}))).
// GIVE THE TOOL A PLACEHOLDER KEY. Practically every API client - openai-python, the
// Anthropic SDK, aws-cli - refuses to start without a key and then sets the auth header
// unconditionally, with no way to turn that off. magus OVERWRITES that header, because
// the value you gave it is garbage by construction. This is the one piece of wiring the
// pattern needs, and leaving it out is why an earlier version of this feature could not
// talk to any real SDK at all.
export fun agent_demo(ctx: magus\Context, args: [str]) > void !> any {
    ctx.skip_cache("opens a credential-carrying endpoint; see api_status");

    os\withEnv({
        "OPENAI_BASE_URL": magus\secret.endpoint(HTTPBIN),
        "OPENAI_API_KEY": "placeholder-magus-replaces-this",
    }, fun () > void {
        // curl stands in for a real agent, and unlike `env` it actually SENDS something -
        // which is the only way this step proves anything. httpbin echoes the request it
        // received, so you can see the Authorization header magus attached and confirm
        // the placeholder above never left this machine.
        proc\exec("curl", args: ["-sS", os\env("OPENAI_BASE_URL") + "/get",
            "-H", "Authorization: Bearer placeholder-magus-replaces-this"], dir: ".");
    });
    magus\log.info("the child sent a request with a placeholder; magus replaced it upstream");
}
